// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/syscall/handlers/io.vuma
// VWK Syscall Handlers — I/O (write, read) (PMT-pure).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the I/O syscall-handler layer. K3e's dispatch.vuma looks up
// the handler for syscall nr (e.g. 64 = __NR_write, 63 = __NR_read) in
// SyscallTable and invokes it. The handler takes the raw u64 args from
// SyscallArgs (a0, a1, a2) and routes them to the appropriate host syscall
// stub. This file implements sys_write and sys_read; sibling proc.vuma
// implements sys_getpid / sys_exit; sibling mm.vuma implements sys_brk /
// sys_mmap / sys_munmap.
//
// ── Why direct extern dispatch (not the SyscallTable indirection)? ──────────
// K3e's dispatch.vuma uses a SyscallTable of u64 handler addresses plus a
// stub-return path (VUMA has no fn-pointer call today — see dispatch.vuma's
// "Why is the handler-call a stub?" header). K3e's stub returns 0; it does
// NOT actually invoke the handler. The handler dispatch is a K11 task (real
// fn-pointer dispatch via a new call_indirect intrinsic or a trap.S switch
// statement).
//
// This file's sys_write / sys_read are the actual handler bodies. K11 will
// register them via syscall_register(tbl, 64, &sys_write) and
// syscall_register(tbl, 63, &sys_read) during kmain init; the dispatch layer
// will then call them. For now (hosted mode), this file is self-testable:
// main() calls sys_write directly to verify the path works end-to-end.
//
// ── Hosted vs bare-metal swap ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// In hosted mode (compile_dump on x86_64), the `write` and `read` externs
// resolve to the pre-registered runtime stubs in
// src/codegen/src/x86_64/mod.rs::build_runtime_syscall_stubs (write =
// syscall #1, read = syscall #0 on x86_64). In bare-metal mode (K11), the
// externs will be re-resolved to MMIO-based UART trampolines (x86_64:
// COM1 serial port at I/O port 0x3F8; aarch64: PL011 UART; riscv64: NS16550
// UART). The handler signatures (sys_write, sys_read) stay byte-identical
// across modes — only the extern block is the swap point. Same pattern as
// womb/kernel/tty/console.vuma re-declaring its `write` extern.
//
// ── ByteBuf layout ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A flat 16-byte buffer used by the self-test. The `data` field is at
// offset 0 so that `buf_state as Address` (cast of the State<ByteBuf> base
// pointer) yields the address of the first buffered byte — the natural
// argument for `write(1, base, count)`. Same pattern as K1d's Console
// layout (buf at offset 0, len following).
//
// ── Why the `buf as Address` cast on a u64 arg works ───────────────────────
// sys_write's signature is `fn sys_write(fd: u64, buf: u64, count: u64) ->
// i64`. The `buf` arg is u64 (the SyscallArgs field type — see K3e's
// abi.vuma SyscallArgs layout, all 7 fields are u64). Inside sys_write, the
// `buf as Address` cast reinterprets the u64 as an Address (raw pointer).
// This is the documented PMT pattern — see probe_addr_cast.vuma and
// probe_io_k3f.vuma (both verified IVE Pass, exit=0). The codegen lowers
// `u64 as Address` to a no-op (the value is already a 64-bit pointer on
// x86_64).
//
// The CALLER (K3e's trap_syscall, once K11 wires it up) passes the user-
// space buffer address as args.a1 (a u64). sys_write reinterprets it as
// an Address and hands it to write(). No bounds-checking is performed —
// K12 (panic/log) will add an user-space-address-validation step.
//
// ── Routing: fd 1 = stdout, fd 2 = stderr, fd 0 = stdin, else VFS ───────────
// sys_write routes by fd:
//   fd == 1 → write(1, buf, count)  (host stdout)
//   fd == 2 → write(2, buf, count)  (host stderr)
//   else    → VFS write (K5 — stub returns 0 for now)
// sys_read routes by fd:
//   fd == 0 → read(0, buf, count)   (host stdin)
//   else    → VFS read (K5 — stub returns 0 for now)
// The VFS path (K5) will replace the `return 0` stub with a real VFS call.
// Until then, only fd 0/1/2 work end-to-end (host console).
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The ByteBuf
// state is allocated via `state_new(ByteBuf)` (which lowers to an Alloc into
// `___pmt_buffer`) in main(). The `buf_state as Address` cast hands the
// state's base pointer to write() — the state remains live in
// `___pmt_buffer` for the duration of the call.
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL per the K3f contract's
// "IMPORTANT" rule:
//     1    = fd for stdout (POSIX STDIN_FILENO = 0, STDOUT_FILENO = 1,
//            STDERR_FILENO = 2)
//     2    = fd for stderr
//     0    = fd for stdin (in sys_read)
//     3    = byte count for the self-test write ('h', 'i', '\n')
//     104  = ASCII 'h'
//     105  = ASCII 'i'
//     10   = ASCII '\n' (newline)
//     16   = ByteBuf.data array size
// Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix width-
// extension subtleties — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for
// the same rationale, and K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule
// applied to vector=14 and rip=4096).
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. The `extern "C" { fn write, fn read }`
// declarations are byte-identical to:
//   - womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/trampoline.vuma (K1a)
//   - womb/kernel/tty/console.vuma (K1d)
//   - womb/kernel/hosted/host.vuma (K1a)
// Future files that want to call sys_write / sys_read (e.g. K4's kmain
// when it bootstraps the syscall table) must re-declare ByteBuf byte-
// identically and either re-declare sys_write/sys_read locally OR wait
// for VUMA to gain an import mechanism.
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/syscall/handlers/io.vuma \
//         /tmp/io.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/io.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0, prints "hi\n" to stdout (3 bytes written).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Extern declarations (byte-identical to K1a's trampoline.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Hosted mode: write = sys_write (syscall #1 on x86_64), read = sys_read
// (syscall #0 on x86_64). Both take (fd, buf, count) in RDI/RSI/RDX and
// return the byte count in RAX. The runtime stub is the canonical
// "mov eax,#num; syscall; ret" sequence — no register shuffling needed
// because all 3 args already match the syscall ABI.
extern "C" {
    fn write(fd: i64, buf: Address, count: i64) -> i64;
    fn read(fd: i64, buf: Address, count: i64) -> i64;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ByteBuf is a flat 16-byte buffer used by the self-test. The `data` field
// is at offset 0 so `buf_state as Address` yields &data[0] — the natural
// argument for write(1, base, count).

layout ByteBuf = {
    data: [u8; 16],
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// sys_write — write `count` bytes from `buf` to fd `fd`.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Routes by fd:
//   fd == 1 → host stdout  (write(1, buf, count))
//   fd == 2 → host stderr  (write(2, buf, count))
//   else    → VFS write (K5 — stub returns 0 for now)
// Returns the byte count written (or a negative errno on failure).
//
// The `buf as Address` cast reinterprets the u64 buffer address as a raw
// pointer (no-op on x86_64 — both are 64-bit). The CALLER (K3e's
// trap_syscall, once K11 wires it up) populates args.a1 with the user-space
// buffer address; sys_write reinterprets it as an Address and hands it to
// the host `write` extern. No bounds-checking — K12 (panic/log) will add
// user-space-address validation.

fn sys_write(fd: u64, buf: u64, count: u64) -> i64 {
    if fd == 1 {
        return write(1, buf as Address, count as i64);
    }
    if fd == 2 {
        return write(2, buf as Address, count as i64);
    }
    // Other fds: VFS write (K5). Stub for now.
    return 0;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// sys_read — read `count` bytes into `buf` from fd `fd`.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Routes by fd:
//   fd == 0 → host stdin  (read(0, buf, count))
//   else    → VFS read (K5 — stub returns 0 for now)
// Returns the byte count read (0 on EOF, negative on error).

fn sys_read(fd: u64, buf: u64, count: u64) -> i64 {
    if fd == 0 {
        return read(0, buf as Address, count as i64);
    }
    // Other fds: VFS read (K5). Stub for now.
    return 0;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate a ByteBuf via state_new, populate the first 3 bytes with
// "hi\n" (ASCII 104, 105, 10), call sys_write(1, &buf, 3) and verify the
// return value is 3 (3 bytes written). Then call sys_read on an invalid
// fd (99) and verify it returns 0 (the VFS-stub path). Returns 0 on
// success, 1 on the write-byte-count mismatch, 2 on the VFS-stub return
// mismatch (so a future CI failure pinpoints the broken path).
//
// The test exercises:
//   - state_new(ByteBuf) — PMT allocation
//   - state.field[idx] = u8 — typed array-store
//   - state as Address — PMT-to-pointer cast (the canonical pattern from
//     K1d's console_flush)
//   - sys_write(1, addr, 3) → write(1, addr, 3) → 3 bytes to stdout
//   - sys_read(99, addr, 3) → returns 0 (VFS stub — fd 99 != 0)
//
// Per the K3f contract's "IMPORTANT" rule: all non-zero constants in the
// self-test use DECIMAL (104, 105, 10, 3, 1, 99) — decimal avoids the
// parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties
// — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale,
// and K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule applied to vector=14
// and rip=4096).

fn main() -> i32 {
    let buf_state = state_new(ByteBuf);
    buf_state.data[0] = 104;  // 'h'
    buf_state.data[1] = 105;  // 'i'
    buf_state.data[2] = 10;   // '\n'
    let buf_addr = buf_state as Address;

    // Test 1: sys_write(1, &buf, 3) → 3 bytes to stdout.
    let ret = sys_write(1, buf_addr, 3);
    if ret != 3 { return 1; }

    // Test 2: sys_read(99, &buf, 3) → VFS stub returns 0 (fd 99 != 0).
    let ret2 = sys_read(99, buf_addr, 3);
    if ret2 != 0 { return 2; }

    return 0;
}
